// The layout tier of the current window, in the contract's own vocabulary.
//
// Six width comparisons used to answer four different questions - "is this a
// phone", "is this window tight", "how many node columns fit" and "does the
// title bar still have room for the traffic capsule" - and each carried its
// own number: 480, 600, 640, 760, 800 and 1120. Two of them sat inside the
// desktop title bar, which only exists above the compact threshold, so they
// could never be false; the rest disagreed with each other by up to 200
// units about where a phone stops being a phone, and every page was free to
// answer the question a sixth way.
//
// One ladder owns the boundaries now, and every consumer asks the ladder
// instead of measuring the window itself. A tier is a single enum variant:
// no consumer can combine two width comparisons into a second, hidden stage
// table, and a new viewport can only be introduced by adding a variant here.
//
// The variant names are the ids the shared contract exports
// (`infiltrator_contract::responsive_viewport::ViewportTier`), because the
// same window asked on two surfaces must get the same answer, in the same
// words. The boundary numbers themselves are not here: they are generated
// into `viewport-ladder.slint` from the contract's exported table, so this
// file names the tier and the ladder decides it.
export enum ViewportTier {
    // A phone: the bottom navigation, no side column, one card per row.
    compact,
    // A narrow window: the labelled sidebar is shed and the grids tighten.
    medium,
    // A normal desktop window: the labelled sidebar and three node columns.
    expanded,
    // An ultra-wide window: the roomy sidebar and the densest grids.
    ultra,
}
